import{_ as a,o as t,c as r,a6 as i}from"./chunks/framework.BTpfZnOf.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"JAX runtime and derivatives","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"jax-runtime.md","filePath":"jax-runtime.md","lastUpdated":null}'),o={name:"jax-runtime.md"};function s(n,e,c,d,l,h){return t(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<h1 id="JAX-runtime-and-derivatives" tabindex="-1">JAX runtime and derivatives <a class="header-anchor" href="#JAX-runtime-and-derivatives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;JAX runtime and derivatives {#JAX-runtime-and-derivatives}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>This guide continues <a href="./jax">JAX source</a>: runtime checks on accelerators, derivative rules in emitted modules and stable rewrites. <a href="./jax-ode">JAX ODE solves</a> covers the ODE bridges.</p><h2 id="Accelerator-runtime-checks" tabindex="-1">Accelerator runtime checks <a class="header-anchor" href="#Accelerator-runtime-checks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Accelerator runtime checks {#Accelerator-runtime-checks}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Data-dependent checks use <code>jax.debug.callback</code>: their predicates are computed on the numerical device and checked on the host. JAX needs a local CPU device for these callbacks, even for valid inputs on CUDA. Start the Python process with <code>JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda,cpu</code>; <code>JAX_PLATFORMS=cuda</code> alone fails when a runtime check executes. CPU-only execution remains supported.</p><p>An explicit platform list makes CUDA initialization mandatory and CUDA the default; it does not silently fall back to CPU if CUDA initialization fails. For a GPU-required consumer, select <code>gpu = jax.devices(&quot;gpu&quot;)[0]</code>, place numerical inputs with <code>jax.device_put(value, gpu)</code>, and verify result and gradient arrays with <code>array.devices() == {gpu}</code>. Evaluate inside <code>with jax.default_device(gpu):</code> so newly created constants use that device too. Numerical function results, including constant terms and numeric leaves of tuple/dict results, are JAX arrays with the emitted dtype. <code>prepare_data</code>, <code>dimension</code>, <code>parameters</code> and module metadata remain host values. The host validates the check predicates; it does not evaluate the numerical kernel. Call <code>jax.block_until_ready(result)</code> and <code>jax.effects_barrier()</code> when checking completion and callback errors. Host callbacks incur transfer and synchronization costs; <code>prepare_data</code> also runs on the host.</p><p>See JAX&#39;s <a href="https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/external-callbacks.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">callback semantics</a> and <a href="https://docs.jax.dev/en/latest/config_options.html#platforms" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">platform configuration</a>. <code>python benchmark/jax_runtime_checks.py</code> verifies the helper&#39;s values, gradients, device placement, invalid-input rejection and missing-GPU failure with a CUDA-enabled JAX installation.</p><h2 id="Derivative-rules" tabindex="-1">Derivative rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#Derivative-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Derivative rules {#Derivative-rules}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>For <code>scalar_derivative_rule</code>, the module generates a <code>jax.custom_jvp</code> adapter from the rule&#39;s mathematical graph. It selects the primal and named partials for the active arguments, then applies the scalar chain rule. An inactive argument&#39;s partial is not contracted with a numeric zero. Calls share their generated helpers, including a joint recipe that serves several activity patterns. Host data preparation uses the primal cut.</p><p>General <code>derivative_rule</code> calls generate adapters from the authored forward branch and activity-selected staged reverse branches. Ordinary <code>jax.grad</code> and <code>jax.value_and_grad</code> compute the joint primal and residuals once, then contract the selected reverse cut. JAX does not differentiate the primal&#39;s convergence loop in place of the supplied derivative. Forward mode uses the authored forward branch; a missing branch or differentiation of a declared data-only input raises. Batching maps whole calls and retains each lane&#39;s lazy branches. These adapters use <code>jax.experimental.hijax.HiPrim</code> and are tested with JAX 0.12.0.1. Only modules containing a general rule import that API.</p><p>Ragged array ports carry their logical lengths through primal, forward and reverse cuts. Lengths have zero tangent; padded cotangents do not contribute to upstream gradients. Runtime checks inside a rule execute only for active calls, including rules reached through a lazy branch or an empty plate. Rules containing array regions retain their source plans as build-time metadata. Native invocation reads only the compiled body and its concrete execution tables.</p><p>The incomplete-beta continued fraction used by distribution tails supplies its value and shape/argument partials through such a shared graph. A first JAX gradient retains those partials rather than a reverse tape of all 300 fraction iterations. The primal and partials use fixed loops, so their higher derivatives remain available through ordinary JAX differentiation.</p><h2 id="Stable-rewrites" tabindex="-1">Stable rewrites <a class="header-anchor" href="#Stable-rewrites" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Stable rewrites {#Stable-rewrites}&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Unless <code>simplify = false</code>, each emitted recipe computes numerically fragile compositions in the stable form native preparation computes, together with the statements that compute its inputs, also from a lazy arm or a plate&#39;s cell: <code>log(exp(u))</code> is emitted as <code>u</code>, and <code>log1p(-p)</code> of <code>p = logistic(u)</code> reads <code>u</code>, so the value and <code>jax.grad</code> stay finite where <code>exp(u)</code> overflows. A law&#39;s term on a value computed outside the law (<code>Normal(0, s)</code>&#39;s <code>log(s)</code> of <code>s = exp(a)</code>) is still translated as written: the law&#39;s endpoint is translated from its compiled lowered code, which the rewrites do not read yet.</p>',13)])])}const m=a(o,[["render",s]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
